# .envrc.example -- copy to .envrc, then run: direnv allow
#
# Requires direnv (https://direnv.net); flixw itself never reads this file.
# direnv exports these into your shell before ./flixw ever starts, which is
# also why it reaches a terminal and not an editor-spawned `flixw lsp`.
#
# One-time setup per machine. direnv does nothing until its hook is in your
# shell's startup file, and until then an .envrc is an inert text file:
#
#   bash   in ~/.bashrc:                  eval "$(direnv hook bash)"
#   zsh    in ~/.zshrc:                   eval "$(direnv hook zsh)"
#   fish   in ~/.config/fish/config.fish: direnv hook fish | source
#
# This file is bash whatever your own shell is: direnv evaluates it with bash
# and exports the difference. So fish users still write `export FOO=bar` here
# -- `set -x FOO bar` is a syntax error in this file.
#
# Everything here is optional and every line is commented out. flixw works with
# none of it set; the full table is in docs/CONTRACT.md.

# ---- which JDK runs the compiler ------------------------------------------
# Prefer pinning it for everyone in .flixw/lock.toml:
#   ./flixw pin <version> --java 21
# That is committed and reproducible. Use this only when *your* machine keeps
# that JDK somewhere the search would not find. An invalid value is fatal, on
# purpose: a silently ignored JDK selection is worse than a stopped build.
#
# export FLIX_JAVA_HOME="$HOME/.sdkman/candidates/java/21.0.5-tem"

# ---- running a compiler flixw did not download ----------------------------
# The jar is NOT digest-verified, every such run says so on stderr, and those
# runs are not stock-compatibility evidence. A valid lock is still required:
# .flixw/lock.toml is read, and drift checked, before the override is.
#
# export FLIX_JAR="$PWD/../flix/build/libs/flix.jar"

# ---- where downloads land -------------------------------------------------
# A cache inside the project, rather than the shared one under your home
# directory. Useful for a throwaway container, or to keep one project's
# compilers off a small home volume; the compiler is then downloaded once
# per project instead of once per machine.
#
# Put it under local/ if you put it here at all -- that is the one path
# .flixw/.gitignore already keeps out of git, and a cache holds a
# ~33MB jar that must never reach a commit.
#
# export FLIX_CACHE_HOME="$PWD/.flixw/local/cache"

# ---- fetching through a mirror --------------------------------------------
# Rewrites the download base only. The pinned digest is unchanged and still
# verified, so a mirror serving different bytes fails rather than substitutes.
# Must be https.
#
# export FLIX_DIST_URL="https://artifacts.example.com/flix"
# export HTTPS_PROXY="http://proxy.example.com:3128"
# export NO_PROXY="localhost,127.0.0.1,.example.com"

# ---- options for the compiler JVM -----------------------------------------
# For a project big enough to need more heap than the default. These go to the
# compiler's JVM, not to flixw's; a deny-list rejects the ones that would
# change what code the JVM loads or runs (-cp, -javaagent:, @argfiles).
#
# export FLIX_JVM_OPTS="-Xmx4g"

# ---- while debugging flixw itself -----------------------------------------
# Per-phase timings on stderr. Transient by nature -- if this is still on in a
# month, that is the sign it belongs in your shell for one command instead.
#
# export FLIXW_TRACE=1

# ---- keeping this file honest ---------------------------------------------
# Anything machine-specific belongs in an ignored file, not in the one you
# might be tempted to commit:
#
# source_env_if_exists .envrc.local
#
# flixw does not edit your .gitignore. Add these yourself if you use them:
#   .envrc
#   .envrc.local
